/* WEB IDL §3.7.6 Attributes' "If attr is not exposed in realm, then continue." and §3.7.7 Operations'
   "If op is not exposed in realm, then continue.", as the one fact those two steps need that no
   identifier states: the global names on which a MEMBER of a [Global] interface may stand.
   A ROW SAYS WHERE THE NAME IS REACHABLE FROM A GLOBAL, not which interface declares it. §3.7.6 puts a
   member of a non-[Global] interface on that interface's PROTOTYPE, so `fetch` is a WorkerGlobalScope
   prototype property in a worker and a Window own property on a page; a row keyed to the [Global]
   interfaces alone would carry Window only and remove it from a worker realm. The set is therefore the
   union over each [Global] interface and every interface it inherits — a SOUND OVER-APPROXIMATION of the
   per-attribute ask, which refuses only a name no interface the realm's global implements declares here.
   A NAME WITH NO ROW IS EXPOSED, as above; a member whose union is `*` or empty is omitted rather than
   emitted, because neither can EXCLUDE and a set of no bits is how `*` is spelled. */
typedef struct IdlMemberExposureRow {
    const char *name;   /* a §3.7.6 attribute or §3.7.7 operation of some §3.3.8 [Global] interface */
    unsigned    set;    /* the global names on which some declaration of that member is exposed */
} IdlMemberExposureRow;

